<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghulam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A ghulam was a slave soldier or servant in Islamic states, trained for military or administrative service and loyal to the ruler. Ghulams served in the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> built a ghulam corps from Caucasian converts to counterbalance the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> tribes.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its core was 10,000 mounted <b>ghulams</b>, slave-soldiers carrying the sher-bacha blunderbuss, a third of them former Shia Qizilbash soldiers of Nader Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ghaznavid army was a professional force paid from the treasury, built around a core of Turkic slave cavalry (<b>ghulams</b>) bound personally to the sultan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ghurid armies combined the infantry of Ghor, known for fighting on foot behind large shields, with Turkic slave cavalry (<b>ghulams</b>), Khalaj, Afghan and other tribal contingents and, in India, captured war elephants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The social hierarchy placed the Qizilbash military aristocracy at the apex in the early period, progressively displaced by Persian bureaucratic families and by the <b>ghulam</b> slave-soldiers through Abbas I's deliberate institutional restructuring.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seljuk army was built around <b>ghulams</b>, professional slave soldiers, predominantly of Turkic origin, trained from youth in cavalry warfare and personally loyal to the sultan or senior commanders, supplemented by Turkmen tribal cavalry and, increasingly, infantry levied from conquered sedentary populations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seljuk army combined Turkmen tribal cavalry, holders of military land grants (iqta), a core of slave soldiers (<b>ghulams</b>) attached to the sultan, and mercenaries, including Frankish and Byzantine soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li></ul></section>
